-- title:
--   Lemma 4.1 — Capped RSG distributional identity and runtime
-- statement:
--   Fix a feasible solution $x$ to $\mathrm{LP}(\theta)$ with $0<\theta<1$, and any integer $N\ge1$. The concrete generators that sample the conditional tentative bundle and run depth-$N$ CapHeight are supported on $\{0,\ldots,N\}^m$. For every buyer and type,
--   $$
--    b_i\ \overset d=\ \min\{N\mathbf1,\mathbf1+\Theta_i(Y^0,Y^1)\}|_{X_i},
--   $$
--   where $X_i\sim\mu_{i,\tau}$ and the two reference profiles are fresh independent draws generated by those same score laws, independently of $X_i$. The equality is equality of the entire score-vector distributions. Each scoring call has expected work bounded by one fixed polynomial in $n,m,2^N$, relative to the tentative-bundle samplers.
--
--   This gives both clauses of Lemma 4.1 using its constructive CapHeight witness, rather than assuming a fixed point or efficient sampler.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: Finite items and nonempty finite buyer type supports in fixed arrival order → Zero-based Fin indices and type supports Fin (k+1), with positive type probabilities; reason: Relabeling covers every source instance, including empty item or buyer sets; zero-probability labels are omitted from support
--   - generalization: An optimal LP(theta) solution with its induced tentative distributions → Every feasible LP(theta) solution, with the same induced distributions and unit sampling access; reason: The recursion, subcriticality and retention arguments use feasibility only; specializing to an optimal solution recovers every printed instance and permits charged approximate preprocessing
--   - encoding: Expected polynomial scoring time given tentative-bundle sampling access → Expected accumulated work of the recursive sampler with explicit local integer/coordinate charges; reason: Local charges cover the stated sampling and threshold operations with fixed polynomial overhead; the goal separately pays for preprocessing and bit-level sampling
--   - encoding: Existence of capped score generators with the distributional identity and efficient implementation → The concrete capRSG family from the source construction satisfies both clauses; reason: Section 4.3 and Lemma 4.4 supply this witness for Lemma 4.1; fixing that witness retains the complete existence claim
-- source:
--   Twan Kroll, Calum MacRury, Rebecca Reiffenhäuser, Siddharth M. Sundaram, STOC 2027 submission #1456 (October 2026), https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-2/1456.pdf, printed p.11, PDF p.12,13, Lemma 4.1.

import Definitions.Def_SubadditiveProphet1456_Quarter_CapHeight
import Definitions.Def_SubadditiveProphet1456_Quarter_Machine

open scoped BigOperators NNReal ENNReal
open MeasureTheory

namespace SubadditiveProphet1456.Quarter

theorem rsg_distribution :
    ∃ (C : ℝ) (d : ℕ), 0 < C ∧
    ∀ (n m : ℕ) (I : Instance n m) (θ : ℝ) (x : Fractional I) (N : ℕ),
      0 < θ → θ < 1 → Feasible I θ x → 1 ≤ N →
      DistributionalEquation I x N (capRSG I x N) ∧
      ∀ (i : Fin n) (t : Fin (I.types i+1)),
        rsgExpectedCost I x N i t ≤ ENNReal.ofReal
          (polyBound C d (n+m+2^N) 0) := by sorry

end SubadditiveProphet1456.Quarter
